    "story": "She was younger than the uniform wanted her to be. The belt fit only because she had cinched it to the last hole, and she wore it the way you wear something borrowed from an older sister — proud of it, and a little afraid of what it promised.\nThe tent smelled of canvas and kerosene, and behind her the kit sat packed the way she had been taught, everything in its place, nothing she would have to search for in the dark.\nBeyond the flap, the ground was waiting for exactly that — someone young enough to run toward the call and steady enough to kneel when she got there.",
    "notes": "What holds this frame is the contrast between the girl and the kit. Her face is barely out of childhood, but the uniform is complete — the cinched belt, the bright Red Cross armband, the packed gear behind her — and the warm painterly light gives all of it the same unhurried respect. She is not posed as a symbol; she is caught in the minute before the work begins, hand resting on her belt, gaze already past the tent. A collector hangs this one for the tension it holds — youth asked to carry an adult's duty, and carrying it without flinching.",
