    "story": "The shift had started before the light did, and it would not end when the light went. She had stopped counting the hours somewhere around the third dressing change, when her hands had learned the work well enough to do it without being told.\nHer cap was still straight. Her apron was not, and she did not care — the men she tended could not see the apron, only the hands, and the hands were steady. The red cross on her cap caught what light there was, and that was enough: a hurting man could find it from across the tent.\nSomewhere behind her, a voice called for a blanket. She would answer it in a moment. First she let herself look down at nothing for exactly one breath, and then she went.",
