    "story": "The coat had stopped keeping the rain out somewhere around the second hour, and after that it had only kept the cold in. He could feel the water finding its way down his collar in a slow, patient line, and he had stopped trying to stop it — some things you do not fight, you outlast.\nThe mud on the coat was the mud of three fields and two roads, and it had dried and wet and dried again until it had become part of the cloth. He did not mind the mud. The mud meant he had been somewhere, and the somewhere was behind him.\nHe smiled because the rain had finally eased to a drizzle, and a drizzle was a thing a man could live with. Tomorrow the coat would dry. Tomorrow the road would dry. For now, the rain on his shoulders was the war, and the war was small enough to wear.",
    "notes": "The coat tells the story before the face does. It is mud to the knees and wet to the shoulders, and the painterly light treats every streak and stain with the same unhurried respect it gives the man's face — which is the point: in this portrait the uniform has a biography of its own, and the biography is written in mud. His slight smile does the rest, turning what could have been a study in misery into a study in outlasting. A collector hangs this one for the weather it keeps — a portrait that does not hide the rain but wears it, and the wearing is what makes the print feel true rather than staged, and true is what holds a wall.",
