    "story": "The roll on his shoulder was not heavy because of what it was. It was heavy because of who it was for — a man lying somewhere ahead, and the getting-there was the job.\nThe armband on his arm had been white once. It was not white anymore; the dust and the sweat had seen to that, and it had become the color of the road it walked. He did not mind. A clean armband was a thing for a parade, and this was not a parade.\nHe kept walking because the walking was the work. The roll would be needed before the light went, and the light was already going.",
    "notes": "Two things carry this frame: the roll and the armband. The roll runs the full height of the figure, a dark line against the pale sky, and the painterly light holds it the way it holds the armband — as a mark of the work, not a prop. The medic's face does the rest: young, tired, and turned toward the road he has not yet walked, with a weariness that says he has already walked a great deal of it. A collector hangs this one for that honesty — a portrait that does not dress the work up, but shows it as it was: a man, a roll, an armband, and a road that does not get shorter for wishing.",
