    "story": "She had picked the hat for the photograph, and the flowers for the hat, and the smile for the boys who would see it — in that order, and on purpose. The photographer had said to look at the lens, and she had, and the looking had done the rest.\nThe boys it was for were a long way off by then, and the distance was the point: a picture is a thing you can carry, and a smile is a thing you can keep, and both of them are lighter than a letter and harder to lose.\nShe did not know which of them would see it first. She knew the hat would still be there when they did, and the flowers would still be in it, and the smile would still be the same one she had practiced in the mirror that morning.",
    "notes": "The hat is doing the talking. It is wide and straw and full of flowers, and the painterly light holds it the way it holds the smile beneath it — as a thing chosen on purpose, for a purpose, and the purpose is written in the title. She is not posing for a portrait; she is posing for the boys, and the difference is everything: the smile is not for us, and the hat is not for us, and the print lets us be the boys for the length of a look. A collector hangs this one for that address — a portrait that knows exactly who it is for, and says so in the title, and the saying is what makes the print feel less like a picture and more like a letter that happens to be a photograph.",
