    "story": "The jeep had been painted for the show before it had been painted for the war. The sign on the front said so, in letters big enough to be read from the back of a crowd, and the rope coiled on the bumper said the driver had learned to carry everything he owned in the space a jeep allows.\nShe was the one they had come to see, and she knew it, and the knowing of it sat easily on her — the way a song sits on a singer who has sung it a hundred times and still means it. The soldiers crowded close, not because the jeep was small, but because the wanting was large.\nThey would drive to the next camp, and the next, and the next, and at every one of them the show would start the same way: a jeep, a sign, a song, and a crowd that had forgotten, for an hour, that there was a war.",
