    "story": "They looked the same way because they had learned to look the same way — not at the camera, and not at each other, but at the ground ahead, where the next thing was already happening.\nThe helmets on their heads wore nets, and the nets wore the dust of the field. The Hampshire on their sleeves was red, and the red was the county, and the county was a long way from this field.\nThey did not speak. The speaking would come later, when the looking was done, and the looking was not done yet.",
    "notes": "Two men, one look. They are both turned the same way, both wearing the red Hampshire on their sleeves, and the painterly light holds the two of them the way it holds one man — as a single body, and as a single gaze. The foreground helmet wears its net the way a man wears a thing he has earned, and the second man is soft behind him, and the softness is the point: the portrait is not about the one in front, but about the two of them together. A collector hangs this one for that pairing — a portrait that proves solidarity, in these pictures, is not a slogan but a shared direction, and the sharing is what the print keeps.",
