    "story": "The goggles stay up on the beret until they are needed, and the beret stays tilted the way it always sits — the same tilt he wore when the hatch was open and the wind came through the turret like a fist. The rifle rides his shoulder the way it has ridden it all day, and the scarf keeps the dust out of his throat, or tries to.\n\nInside the steel the air tastes of oil and hot metal, and the crew learn to talk in half-sentences because the engine says the rest. Out here the quiet is the strange part. He watches the lens the way a gunner watches a hedgerow — patient, and half expecting the day to move before he does.",
    "notes": "The black beret does the work in this frame — tilted just so, with the goggles pushed up above it, it gives the whole portrait its attitude before a word of the story is read. The weathered stone wall behind him and the camouflage smock at his shoulders add texture without taking the eye off the face, and that wry, level look is the kind of expression that only comes from a man who has done this before. It is a tight, confident portrait of the armoured crewman at rest, and it hangs with an easy authority.",
