    "story": "She picks the second verse because it is the verse the boys always join in on, even the shy ones at the back. The microphone is cold against her lip and smells faintly of metal and the last singer's powder, and the trees hold the sound longer than any hall ever did.\n\nShe is not singing for the men in front of her, not really. She is singing for the letters they will write tonight, for the kitchen radios back home, for the version of this afternoon they will carry into whatever comes next. The red dress is her own small defiance — bright on purpose, because a grey day does not deserve a grey song.",
    "notes": "The red floral dress is the frame's anchor — bright and unapologetic against the soft green trees, it pulls the eye straight to her profile and the chrome microphone at her lips. The painted light on her hair and the smile caught mid-phrase make this less a portrait of a singer than a portrait of the song itself, which is exactly why it hangs so well. A warm, rare civilian counterpoint to the uniforms of the set, and a reminder that the war was also fought with a stage and a microphone.",
