    "story": "The pack settles into his shoulders with every step, and he lets it. The rifle rides his back, the pouches knock against his hip in time with the boots, and the dust gets into everything — his throat, his collar, the corners of his eyes — before the column has gone a mile.\n\nHe does not count the miles any more. He counts the ridge lines, the hedgerows, the places where the road might bend and give him something to look at besides the back of the man in front. Somewhere ahead the column stops, the packs come off, and somebody gets a fire going. Until then it is the step, and the step after it, and the road that does not care how tired his legs are.",
    "notes": "Mid-stride is where this frame earns its keep — the rifle slung, the pouches knocking at his hip, the dust caught in the painted air around him. The profile view keeps the composition simple and the eye on the march itself, and the haze softens the whole scene into something closer to memory than reportage. It is a strong, kinetic portrait of the infantryman's daily distance, and it brings movement to any wall it hangs on.",
