    "story": "The bag knocks against her hip with every step, and she has stopped trying to quiet it — the man on the ground will hear her coming either way, and hearing her coming is half the treatment. The red cross on her arm stands out against the brown of her battledress, and that is the point: a wounded man has to be able to find it through the smoke and the mud.\n\nShe is not running toward the sound. She is running toward the silence after it — the place where a man has stopped calling out and started waiting, and waiting is the worst of it. The field is soft under her boots and the air smells of wet grass and something worse, and every step is a small argument with the ground. She wins it by keeping her eyes on the place where the grass is pressed flat, where somebody went down and stayed down.",
    "notes": "Motion is what makes this frame — she is caught mid-stride, leaning into the run, the medical bag swinging at her shoulder and the red cross on her arm the single clean note of colour against the khaki and the pale field. The composition keeps her off-centre and the open ground ahead of her, so the eye runs with her toward the casualty it cannot see. It is a rare, urgent portrait of the medic at work, and it brings a pulse to any wall it hangs on.",
