    "story": "The cap sits where it has sat since the morning — tilted, neat, the way she was taught to wear it — and the bun at the back of her neck is pulled tight enough to last the day. She keeps her face in profile because the camera is not what she is looking at; she is looking past it, at the letter she has written in her head and not yet put to paper.\n\nYesterday's letter went off with the evening post, full of the small news she is allowed to send and none of the news she wants to. Today's will say the same, in different words, because the things that matter do not fit in an envelope, and the people who would understand them are not there to read it. She keeps her face turned away and lets the paper behind her hold the rest.",
    "notes": "The profile carries this one — the side cap, the bun, the clean line of her jaw against a backdrop of faded handwriting, all of it held in a single quiet register. The painted letters behind her turn a simple portrait into a story about the words she is not saying, and the warm tones keep the frame gentle instead of stern. It is a soft, contemplative piece in a set full of uniforms, and it rewards a long look.",
