    "story": "He takes the helmet off because the air has gone still and the weight of it on his head is the argument he is tired of losing. It hangs from his hand now, light as a bowl, and the rifle stands beside him muzzle-down in the dust, the way a man leans a tool when the work has stopped for a breath.\n\nThe field is empty in every direction, and he has stopped asking where everybody went — the column is ahead or behind, and either way the ground under his boots is his to stand on for a minute. Yesterday there was a road and a village and a song somebody was singing. Today there is the pale sky, the cracked earth, and a man deciding whether to put the helmet back on, or to carry it a little longer.",
    "notes": "The empty ground is what gives this frame its weight — one man, full-length, centred in a pale field that offers nothing to hide behind, with the helmet in his hand and the rifle at his side. The composition is almost a study in solitude: muted sepia tones, a low horizon, and a figure who looks as though he has been standing there a long time and will stand there a while longer. It is a quiet, monumental portrait of the lone infantryman, and it holds a wall with an ease that busier frames can't match.",
