    "story": "The street has gone quiet in the way streets go quiet when they are listening, and he does not trust the quiet — he trusts the rifle in his hands, and the pack on his back, and the man behind him who has not said a word in ten minutes. The pack pulls at his shoulders with every step, and the entrenching tool knocks against his hip, and he has stopped noticing either of them, the way you stop noticing a tooth that has ached all morning.\n\nYesterday the street had laundry on a line and a dog that barked at the column. Today the line is empty and the dog is gone, and the windows are shut the way windows shut when the men of the house have gone. He keeps his eyes on the corner ahead, and his feet on the cobbles, and the rifle where it belongs — up and ready, because the corner is where the war keeps its appointments.",
    "notes": "The readiness is what carries this frame — the rifle up in both hands, the pack riding high, the entrenching tool at his hip, all of it held in a posture that says he has not lowered his guard since the morning. The painted street behind him stays soft and uncluttered, so the eye stays on the advance itself, and the warm, dusty tones give the whole scene the feeling of a day that has been going on for a long time. It is a strong, alert portrait of the infantryman on the move, and it brings a sense of purpose to any wall it hangs on.",
