    "story": "The suit does not belong in a camp, and he knows it — that is the point of it. He wears the suit so the men know he is not one of them and not pretending to be, and the fedora sits on his head the way it sat on the train, the way it sat in the office, the way it will sit when he goes home and tells his wife what the camp looks like.\n\nHe has come with something in his hand and a piece of news in his pocket, and he is not sure which of the two matters more — the thing is small and the news is smaller, and both of them have to be delivered to men who are used to bigger things arriving by truck. Yesterday the tent was a place he had only read about. Today it is canvas and rope and the smell of damp earth, and he stands in front of it the way a man stands in front of a door he has knocked on, waiting to be let in.",
    "notes": "The suit is the surprise in this one — dark, three-piece and immaculate against the canvas of the tent, it sets the frame apart from every uniform in the set before the viewer has read a word of the story. The fedora sits at its familiar tilt and the small yellow object in his hand gives the portrait its quiet mystery, the kind of detail that starts a conversation. It is a rare, gentle portrait of the civilian who came to the war, and it brings a different kind of gravity to any wall it hangs on.",
