    "story": "The ribbons on her chest are not a decoration. They are a record, written in color and cloth, of the years she gave and the places she went — and she wears them the way a woman wears a good coat, without fuss and without apology. The glasses perch on her nose and the cap sits on her hair the way they always have, and she smiles the way a woman who has seen the worst of it smiles at the morning.\n\nShe has worn the uniform longer than most of the men in the camp, and she has seen it change — the cut of the coat, the weight of the cloth, the letters on the lapels — and none of it has changed the work. The work is the same as it was when she first put the cap on: the papers, the people, the day held together by a steady hand. She will go back to it when the camera is done, and the ribbons will go with her, because a record is not something you take off with the uniform.",
    "notes": "The ribbons are what the eye finds first — two neat rows of color on the olive-drab coat, the evidence of a long service worn without fuss, and they turn a simple head-and-shoulders portrait into a record of a life. The garrison cap, the glasses and the steady smile give the frame its warmth, and the painted light keeps the whole portrait in a soft, respectful register. It is one of the set's most dignified images of the women who served, and it holds a wall with the quiet authority of its subject.",
