    "story": "The knot is not quite right, and she knows it the moment her fingers find it — the bandage has slipped while he slept, and the edge of it is coming loose against his cheek. She works it back into place with the care of a woman folding a letter, because a bandage that holds is the difference between a man who heals and a man who does not, and she has learned to put her attention where it matters.\n\nHe watches her hands while she works, because there is nothing else to watch and because watching her hands is easier than watching the tent roof. Yesterday the bandage went on in a hurry, with the guns still talking somewhere beyond the canvas. Today the guns are quieter, and she has time to do it properly, and the time is the gift — a slow minute, a careful knot, a man who can breathe without wincing, and a nurse who knows that the small things hold a man together.",
    "notes": "The hands are the story of this frame — the nurse's hands at the bandage, steady and practiced, and the wounded man's eyes on them, trusting what they know. The white of the bandages and her headscarf sets the light of the portrait against the gray-green of his uniform, and the composition keeps the pair close, so the viewer is a third person in a very small, quiet room. It is a tender, intimate portrait of the nursing that happens behind the fighting, and it brings a stillness to any wall it hangs on.",
