    "story": "The rocks are not comfortable, and nobody pretends they are — they are rough and warm from the sun, and they put a stone under each of them in a different place, and the three of them agree without saying it that a rock you sit on is better than a chair you stand beside. The sea is behind them, going about its business, and the sunglasses are on because the glare is off the water, and the day is for sitting, not for watching.\n\nYesterday they stood in a line on the same sand while the camera did its work, and the line was for the record. Today the record is done and the rocks are for them, and they sit the way women sit when nobody is counting — knees together, hands in their laps, the boots kicked out in front of them the way tired boots get kicked out. The photograph will go home all the same, and the people at home will see three women on a beach, and they will not know about the stones. That is the secret they keep, and it makes them smile.",
    "notes": "The rocks give this one its charm — three women in service dress, perched on sun-warmed stone with the sea behind them, their boots kicked out and one pair of sunglasses catching the light. The composition is looser than a parade-ground line, and that looseness is the point: three women off duty, at ease, and in no hurry to be anywhere else. It is a warm, companionable portrait of the war's women at rest, and it brings a seaside ease to any wall it hangs on.",
