    "story": "The apron says Victory in big letters, and the woman wearing it means every one of them — she has put the dress on for the photograph and the apron over it on purpose, because the home front is a front too, and it deserves its uniform. The woman beside her wears a different uniform: a flight suit and a leather helmet, with the goggles pushed up on her forehead and the sunglasses on, the way a woman who has flown wears the sky.\n\nYesterday the pilot was in the air, and the air was the place where the war made sense to her. Yesterday the other woman was in a kitchen, and the kitchen was the place where the war made sense to her. Today they are side by side in front of the camera, and the camera does not care which of them did more — the photograph only knows that the war needed them both, and the apron and the flight suit are the two kinds of proof.",
    "notes": "The pairing is what makes this frame — the flight suit on one side and the Victory apron on the other, the leather helmet and goggles against the patterned dress, two uniforms that have nothing in common and everything in common. The sepia light holds the pair in a warm, even register, and the two women sit close enough that the viewer reads them as a team. It is a rare double portrait of the war's women in two roles, and it brings a sense of partnership to any wall it hangs on.",
